<% content_for :title, "DBT & Addiction | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="dbt-addiction-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="dbt-addiction-hero">

    <picture class="dbt-addiction-background">
      <source
        media="(max-width: 767px)"
        srcset="<%= asset_path('addiction_dbt_mobile.png') %>">

      <%= image_tag "addiction_dbt_desktop.png",
          alt: "",
          class: "dbt-addiction-background-image",
          fetchpriority: "high" %>
    </picture>

    <div class="dbt-addiction-hero-content">

      <p class="dbt-addiction-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ADDICTION / PERSPECTIVES / DBT
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>ADDICTION</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>DBT LENS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="dbt-addiction-intro">
        ACCEPT WHAT IS.<br>
        BUILD WHAT COMES NEXT.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 01 / OPENING -->
  <section class="da-section da-pink">

    <div class="da-heading">
      <p class="da-number">01 / ANOTHER WAY TO LOOK</p>

      <h2>
        TWO THINGS<br>
        CAN BE<br>
        TRUE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="da-copy">
<p class="da-lead">DBT holds two truths at once: you can understand why alcohol appealed to you and still take responsibility for what followed.</p>
<p>Physical pain, mania, and emotional distress influenced your decisions. None of those circumstances erase the consequences. Acceptance means acknowledging what happened accurately; change means examining what you can do differently.</p>
<div class="da-callout">ACCEPTANCE & CHANGE.<br>ROOM FOR BOTH.</div>
    

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 02 / DIALECTICS -->
  <section class="da-section da-yellow">

    <div class="da-heading">
      <p class="da-number">02 / ACCEPTANCE & CHANGE</p>

      <h2>
        START HERE.<br>
        MAKE ROOM<br>
        TO CHANGE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="da-copy">
<p class="da-lead">You can experience genuine suffering and recognize that the way you responded caused harm.</p>
<p>During your First DUI, your sciatica pain and anger were real, but drinking while driving put others at risk. During your Third DUI, mania impaired judgment without making the consequences disappear. DBT asks you to hold both sides of these experiences rather than deny either one.</p>
<div class="da-grid"><div class="da-box"><h3>ACCEPTANCE</h3><p>Recognize the facts, the pain, and the influence of your mental state.</p></div><div class="da-box"><h3>CHANGE</h3><p>Own the behavior, seek support, and make a plan to reduce future harm.</p></div></div>
    

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 03 / MINDFULNESS -->
  <section class="da-section da-purple">

    <div class="da-heading">
      <p class="da-number">03 / MINDFULNESS & WISE MIND</p>

      <h2>
        NOTICE<br>
        WHAT'S<br>
        HERE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="da-copy">
<p class="da-lead">Emotion Mind reacts to urgency. Reasonable Mind considers facts. Wise Mind makes room for both.</p>
<p>When the interlock malfunctioned during your Third DUI, <em>Jackpot</em> was the excited impulse toward drinking. <em>No</em> was the wiser warning you ignored. You recognized your mania, but recognition alone wasn't enough to regulate your behavior as the episode progressed.</p>
<div class="da-grid"><div class="da-box"><h3>EMOTION MIND</h3><p>The immediate pull of relief, excitement, anger, or fear.</p></div><div class="da-box"><h3>REASONABLE MIND</h3><p>The facts, risks, and practical consequences.</p></div></div>
<div class="da-callout">WISE MIND.<br>HEAR BOTH BEFORE YOU ACT.</div>
    

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 04 / EMOTION REGULATION -->
  <section class="da-section da-black">

    <div class="da-heading">
      <p class="da-number">04 / EMOTION REGULATION</p>

      <h2>
        UNDERSTAND<br>
        WHAT YOU<br>
        FEEL.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="da-copy">
<p class="da-lead">A reaction rarely begins with the final impulsive act. Vulnerability builds long before it.</p>
<p>Your severe sciatica, isolation, and anger increased vulnerability before your First DUI. Restlessness, reduced sleep, mania, and secret drinking preceded your Third DUI. Insecurities and intoxication intensified the emotions behind Press Send. A DBT behavior chain examines the conditions, prompting events, thoughts, urges, actions, and consequences rather than treating each incident as one inexplicable moment.</p>
<div class="da-stack"><div class="da-stack-item"><span>01</span><div><h3>VULNERABILITY</h3><p>What was already making the situation harder?</p></div></div><div class="da-stack-item"><span>02</span><div><h3>THE CHAIN</h3><p>What happened between the first urge and the behavior?</p></div></div><div class="da-stack-item"><span>03</span><div><h3>THE INTERRUPT</h3><p>Where could support, delay, or another action change the outcome?</p></div></div></div>
    

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 05 / DISTRESS TOLERANCE -->
  <section class="da-section da-pink">

    <div class="da-heading">
      <p class="da-number">05 / DISTRESS TOLERANCE</p>

      <h2>
        SOME THINGS<br>
        TAKE<br>
        TIME.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="da-copy">
<p class="da-lead">Distress tolerance means getting through a crisis without adding another crisis. It does not mean pretending untreated pain is manageable.</p>
<p>You had already endured months of severe pain before your First DUI. You needed medical care as well as coping skills. With mania, the most useful intervention may come earlier—when sleep changes, restlessness increases, or drinking becomes secretive—rather than relying on willpower once judgment is severely impaired.</p>
<div class="da-grid"><div class="da-box"><h3>PAUSE</h3><p>Delay a risky decision when you still have room to choose.</p></div><div class="da-box"><h3>GROUND</h3><p>Recognize the emotion and the physical state underneath it.</p></div><div class="da-box"><h3>SEEK SUPPORT</h3><p>Use a relapse or crisis plan before things escalate.</p></div><div class="da-box"><h3>REASSESS</h3><p>Address the underlying pain, symptoms, or practical problem.</p></div></div>
    

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 06 / RELATIONSHIPS -->
  <section class="da-section da-yellow">

    <div class="da-heading">
      <p class="da-number">06 / INTERPERSONAL EFFECTIVENESS</p>

      <h2>
        SAY WHAT<br>
        YOU FUCKING<br>
        NEED.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="da-copy">
<p class="da-lead">Your feelings can be valid even when the way you express them is harmful.</p>
<p>In Press Send, you wanted people to know you were hurting and needed them. Sometimes you tried to communicate that by hurting them in return. You respected boundaries when they were explicitly established, but losing contact still brought shame, guilt, and sorrow. DBT's interpersonal skills make space for clear requests, care for the relationship, and self-respect—without demanding a particular response.</p>
<div class="da-grid"><div class="da-box"><h3>DEAR MAN</h3><p>Describe, Express, Assert, Reinforce; stay Mindful, Appear confident, Negotiate.</p></div><div class="da-box"><h3>GIVE</h3><p>Be Gentle, Interested, Validating, and use an Easy manner.</p></div><div class="da-box"><h3>FAST</h3><p>Be Fair, avoid excessive Apologies, Stick to values, and be Truthful.</p></div><div class="da-box"><h3>BOUNDARIES</h3><p>Apologize when needed, and respect the other person's decision.</p></div></div>
    

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 07 / BUILDING A LIFE -->
  <section class="da-section da-purple">

    <div class="da-heading">
      <p class="da-number">07 / A LIFE WORTH LIVING</p>

      <h2>
        MAKE ROOM<br>
        FOR THE<br>
        GOOD SHIT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="da-copy">
<p class="da-lead">Recovery isn't only about preventing the next harmful decision. It's also about creating a life worth living without alcohol.</p>
<p>DBT combines acceptance, emotion regulation, practical support, and repeated skill practice. You can take responsibility like an adult without pretending pain, bipolar symptoms, or interpersonal distress had no influence. Connection, creativity, competence, and enjoyment still matter.</p>
<div class="da-grid"><div class="da-box"><h3>CONNECTION</h3><p>Relationships that allow honesty and mutual respect.</p></div><div class="da-box"><h3>CREATIVITY</h3><p>Activities that offer something beyond immediate relief.</p></div><div class="da-box"><h3>COMPETENCE</h3><p>Practicing skills while your judgment is clear.</p></div><div class="da-box"><h3>ENJOYMENT</h3><p>Making room for pleasure that doesn't create new harm.</p></div></div>
    

    </div>

  </section>



  <!-- 08 / ALCOHOL DIARIES -->
  <section class="da-section da-black" id="alcohol-diaries">
    <div class="da-heading">
      <p class="da-number">08 / ALCOHOL DIARIES</p>
      <h2>THREE<br>STORIES.<br>ONE LENS.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="da-copy">
      <p class="da-lead">DBT looks at what made you vulnerable, what happened next, and where the chain could have changed.</p>
      <div class="da-stack">
        <div class="da-stack-item"><span>01</span><div><h3>FIRST DUI</h3><p>Severe sciatica, anger, and isolation left you desperate for relief. Drinking briefly reduced the pain and anger, reinforcing the urge to keep going. There were points in the chain where you might have stopped, but distress tolerance alone was not a substitute for treating the underlying pain.</p></div></div>
        <div class="da-stack-item"><span>02</span><div><h3>THIRD DUI</h3><p>Mania, restlessness, and secret drinking formed a chain before the interlock malfunctioned. <em>Jackpot</em> reflected Emotion Mind; <em>No</em> reflected Wise Mind. Earlier recognition of escalating symptoms and a crisis plan could have offered support before your judgment became more impaired.</p></div></div>
        <div class="da-stack-item"><span>03</span><div><h3>PRESS SEND</h3><p>Insecurities and intoxication intensified your emotional distress. You wanted others to understand your pain, but sometimes communicated it by trying to hurt them. DBT offers alternatives for expressing needs, and repair may mean apologizing and leaving someone alone if that's what they want.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="dbt-addiction-ending">

    <div class="da-ending-heading">

      <p class="da-ending-small">
        UNDERSTANDING. PRACTICE. PARTICIPATION.
      </p>

      <h2>
        ACCEPT.<br>
        PRACTICE.<br>
        <span>BUILD.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="da-ending-copy">

      <p>
        DBT reveals how vulnerability, emotional intensity, and impulsivity contribute to harmful behavior. You can understand what led to an action without excusing its consequences.
      </p>

      <p>
        Acceptance isn't approval. Responsibility doesn't require denying your suffering. The work is to recognize the patterns early, use support, and respond without creating more harm.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO ADDICTION PERSPECTIVES",
          addiction_perspectives_path,
          class: "da-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>